<?xml version="1.0"?>
<ScenContainer xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">
  <ScenTitle>The Libyan War, 1986</ScenTitle>
  <ScenDescription>&lt;BODY scroll=auto&gt;&lt;SPAN lang=EN&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;The Libyan War, 1986&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;Al "Beirutdude" Sandrik&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;The Time Is: 13 April 1986, 18:00 Zulu/19:00 Tripoli Time&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;Designed: December 2020 C:MO V1.02 Build 1174.14&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;Central Mediterranean Sea/Libya&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;Playable as France-US&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;Duration: Up to 5 Days and 6 Hours&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;Warning: This is absolutely NOT a "balanced" scenario, it is more of a simulation! Libya has absolutely no chance to win. The balance/challenge is derived through the scoring. In real life the US and France would have beat up on Libya and that is exactly what happens here, so the challenge is to do it well and avoid losses. If you don't like that kind of scenario then, honestly, this one is not for you.&lt;/STRONG&gt;　&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;Background:&lt;/STRONG&gt; On 5 April 1986, three people were killed and 229 injured when the La Belle discothèque was bombed in the Friedenau District of West Berlin. The entertainment venue was commonly frequented by United States soldiers, two of the dead and 79 of the injured were Americans. This attack followed a long reign of terror and military operations against both the United States and France and was traced back to Libyan Intelligence. Indeed, France was actively engaged in direct military confrontations with Colonel Muammar Gaddafi’s forces in Chad, the Sudan and Niger.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;As America planned its response, French diplomats were approached with a request for American F-111 aircraft and tankers to transit their airspace, but instead of approval they received a surprising response of "NO!" France was upset over the American’s bailing out of the revenge strikes in Lebanon two and a half years earlier. Additionally, France wanted more than another pinprick raid, they wanted Gaddafi gone. France offered not only her airbases but direct French participation　for an operation oriented toward regime change. &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;The Reagan Administration, already knew regime change was a non-starter with the Soviet Union, but they also knew the USSR would look the other way for operations short of that goal. Indeed, the Soviet Ambassador to the United States told his counterpart, "We understand he has gone to far this time, Go ahead, get it out of your system, but understand we will not countenance a regime change. Take a few days to make your point, but be careful not to go too far! Honestly this is better for us, he has his oil money and we will make a tidy profit rebuilding his forces. Do not impact any Soviet property and maintain perspective and we will not take more than the usual protests in the Security Council." &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;With this information, which France was already aware of, the Americans approached the French with a proposed 3-to-4-day operation to destroy Libyan forces and if any of the Gaddafi command staff were killed, well that’s just the price of business! France, pragmatically agreed to a scaled back operation and requested American tanker support which was promptly agreed to.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;Over the next few days, Diplomats looked for support from other nations and French and American forces began to move. The diplomatic efforts were a mixed bag:&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;France began to stockpile munitions and move aircraft to the five (5) long runway bases in Corsica. With tanker support they could hit the French sector west of Tripoli. The French aircraft carrier Clemenceau, with escorts and support vessels, departed Naval Base Toulon and French submarines headed toward the central Med. &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;Israel agreed to supply three airbases in the central part of the county　with munitions as long as America agreed to make good on the stockpiles after the war, a pay back for American support in 1973. Elements of the USMC’s 2nd Marine Air Wing (MAW) and a few USAF squadrons (with transports bringing in Precision Guided Munitions [PGMs]) deployed to Israel. &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;The United Kingdom agreed to the use of RAF bases in the UK and RAF Akrotiri but would not directly participate in operations. &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;Turkey, would not participate but would not restrict "normal operations" from the Incirlik Air Base as long as they were restricted to support missions only. &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;Likewise, Italy would allow "normal" use of NAS Sigonella for MPA aircraft and the American Naval Base in Gaeta for supply, but would not allow any other support or overflights from Italian soil. &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;Greece, was a bit more liberal and would allow several USAF squadrons to operate from the Souda Bay Air Base, but no other base, nor could American naval vessels use Souda Bay for support. &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;Spain, would not allow any overflights or the use of bases on their soil in any fashion. &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;Malta also denied the use of Maltese airfields, and indeed, Maltese Prime Minister Karmenu Mifsud Bonnici (and Italian Prime Minister Bettino Craxi as well) ensured Gaddafi was fully briefed on the French-American communications with　his government. &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;The USS Saratoga CVBG was recalled from the Atlantic and on the afternoon of 13 April had just reentered the Med. Redesignated CTF 60.3 they were speeding toward Libya. The USS America CVBG (CTF 60.1) was just west of Crete and the USS Coral Sea CVBG (CTF 60.2) was returning from a port call in Palma, Spain. &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;As was mentioned before, USAF transports and aircraft from the Civilian Reserve Air Fleet (CRAF) were disgorging arms and support personnel for the USAF/USMC forward deployed squadrons. Due to the NATO defense of NORTHAG/CENTAG no USAF aircraft were deployed from central Europe with air assets coming from the CONUS based Nineth Tactical Air Force and Air National Guard. &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;By the evening of the 13th (18:00 utc) most units, other than some ships still deploying. were in position. &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;The first strikes are scheduled to commence after 18:00 utc 14 April, 1986, so arm your aircraft appropriately! &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;Notes:&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;Please ensure your Pop-Up Messages are activated, there could be a few messages.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;Aircraft squadrons at their home base and/or on a carrier have aircraft in maintenance status, aircraft forward deployed have left the hangar queens at home (note the "odd numbers" of aircraft in some squadrons and lack of sequential numbering).&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;For the most part, I let you arm the strike aircraft. You will not have enough PGMs and will need to resort to dumb bombs so use your PGMs wisely. You have one day of game time to arm your aircraft, set missions and get units into position. I do arm the support and ASW aircraft, feel free to change them if you like, but remember with sustained air operations they don't turn around nearly as quickly. So, I have spread out the ASW Helios/MPA and S3As　through the first day. &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;AAW/CAP: I have also armed one squadron for AAW/CAP on each carrier. I just can’t imagine a USN carrier with no CAP/Alert Birds available for 20 hours! &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;Honestly, this scenario is the long haul, it is intended to showcase extended duration operations. It’s not necessary for your aircraft to fly constantly! Use your aircraft well, but you really don’t need to have 250 strike aircraft flying a Time on Target strike at 00:01 15 April! I did turn off surge so think sustained operations! I armed B-52H's (with 1 day, 20-hour ready time)&amp;nbsp;and set to 20 hours ready time, if you change their loadouts, I suggest you do so in the Scenario Editor.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;The Soviet units are "tattle tails/tales" and will (should) not attack you but will provide Libya with intelligence, DO NOT ATTACK THEM! Bad things happen scoring wise for destroying Soviet Units!&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;Speaking of scoring, you lose points for loosing units, the American and French public are not going to support you if you take significant losses of their sons and brothers!&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;You do receive Victory Points for beating up Libyan units. Your mission is to damage and destroy Libyan infrastructure. By the way, your NATO partners may not appreciate your destroying Libyan oil infrastructure, oh well!!!!!!! Oil infrastructure is worth VPs!&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;Some Libyan vessels in port cannot get underway during the duration of the scenario. They are the ship equivalent of aircraft down for maintenance at an airbase. Kill them!!!!!&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;Some bases have restrictions on aircraft that can use them, other than in an emergency landing/diversion, please respect those limits indicated in the base name.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;The No Navigation Zones are set for aircraft only. Vessels can transit them (for example in Italy ships can sail to Gaeta). &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;Feel free to end the scenario when you feel you have completed your mission objectives. &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;You have plenty of reconnaissance assets at your disposal. In fact, you have plenty of aircraft at your disposal. I really suggest looking toward sustained operations after an initial surge for some specialized units such as Suppression of Enemy Air Defenses (SEAD) missions. That said, have fun doing it your way commander!!!!&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;Both .INI and .Delta files are included &lt;/STRONG&gt;with the Zip File for rebuilds &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;Originally created: Command: Modern Operations (C:MO) C:MO V1.02 Build 1174.14&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;Updated: TBD&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;Al "BeirutDude" Sandrik (ex-Beachinnole)&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT color=white&gt;Al.Sandrik@gmail.com&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/BODY&gt;</ScenDescription>
